# AbleSharp.SDK - High-Level API Rules

## Purpose
AbleSharp.SDK provides a high-level, developer-friendly API for integrating AbleSharp functionality into applications. It wraps the low-level AbleSharp.Lib classes with a cleaner, more intuitive interface.

## Architecture

### Project Structure
```
AbleSharp.SDK/
├── Core/           # Core handlers (ProjectHandler, Merger, Dumper, Factory)
├── Options/        # Configuration options classes
├── Utils/          # Utility classes
└── AbleSharpSdk.cs # Main SDK entry point (singleton)
```

## Design Philosophy

### Facade Pattern
- SDK provides a simplified facade over the complex core library
- Hide implementation details from consumers
- Provide sensible defaults
- Make common tasks easy, complex tasks possible

### Singleton Pattern
```csharp
public class AbleSharpSdk
{
    private static readonly Lazy<AbleSharpSdk> _instance = new(() => new AbleSharpSdk());
    public static AbleSharpSdk Instance => _instance.Value;
    
    private AbleSharpSdk() { /* Private constructor */ }
}
```
- Use thread-safe lazy initialization
- Single point of access for SDK functionality
- Stateless design (store no mutable state in singleton)

### Options Pattern
All SDK methods should accept options objects:
```csharp
public AbletonProject OpenProject(string path, ProjectOpenOptions? options = null)
{
    options ??= new ProjectOpenOptions();
    // Implementation
}
```

## Core Components

### 1. AbleSharpSdk (Main Entry Point)
```csharp
public class AbleSharpSdk
{
    // Properties
    public List<AbletonVersion> AvailableVersions { get; }
    public AbletonVersion LatestVersion { get; }
    
    // Core Operations
    public AbletonProject CreateProject(ProjectCreationOptions? options = null);
    public AbletonProject OpenProject(string path, ProjectOpenOptions? options = null);
    public void SaveProject(AbletonProject project, string path, ProjectSaveOptions? options = null);
    public void ExportProject(AbletonProject project, string path, AbletonVersion targetVersion, ProjectExportOptions? options = null);
    public AbletonProject MergeProjects(IEnumerable<AbletonProject> projects, ProjectMergeOptions? options = null);
    public string GetProjectDump(AbletonProject project);
    public bool IsVersionSupported(AbletonVersion version);
}
```

### 2. Options Classes
All options classes should:
- Have nullable optional parameters
- Provide sensible defaults
- Include `Logger` delegate for feedback
- Include `ErrorHandling` enum for behavior control

```csharp
public class ProjectOpenOptions
{
    public ErrorHandling ErrorHandling { get; set; } = ErrorHandling.ThrowException;
    public Action<string>? Logger { get; set; }
    // Other options...
}

public enum ErrorHandling
{
    ThrowException,  // Default: throw on errors
    ReturnEmpty      // Return empty project on errors
}
```

### 3. Core Handler Classes

#### AbletonProjectHandler
- Responsible for loading and saving projects
- Handles file I/O, compression, validation
- Provides static methods for common operations
```csharp
public static class AbletonProjectHandler
{
    public static AbletonProject LoadFromFile(string path);
    public static void SaveToFile(AbletonProject project, string path);
    // Other methods...
}
```

#### AbletonProjectFactory
- Creates new projects from scratch
- Provides templates and defaults
```csharp
public static class AbletonProjectFactory
{
    public static AbletonProject CreateBlankProject();
    public static AbletonProject CreateFromTemplate(string templateName);
    // Other methods...
}
```

#### AbletonProjectMerger
- Merges multiple projects into one
- Handles conflicts, track ordering, scene merging
```csharp
public static class AbletonProjectMerger
{
    public static AbletonProject MergeProjects(List<AbletonProject> projects);
    // Other methods...
}
```

#### AbletonProjectDumper
- Generates text representations of projects
- Useful for debugging and testing
```csharp
public static class AbletonProjectDumper
{
    public static string DebugDumpProject(AbletonProject project);
    // Other methods...
}
```

### 4. AbletonVersion Class
```csharp
public class AbletonVersion(string displayName, string minorVersion, string namespaceVersion)
{
    public string DisplayName { get; } = displayName;      // e.g., "12.0"
    public string MinorVersion { get; } = minorVersion;    // e.g., "12.0.0"
    public string NamespaceVersion { get; } = namespaceVersion; // e.g., "v12_0_0"
}
```

## Coding Guidelines

### API Design Principles

1. **Fluent and Intuitive**
```csharp
// Good: Clear, readable
var project = sdk.OpenProject("project.als");
sdk.SaveProject(project, "output.als");

// Bad: Complex, unclear
var handler = new ProjectHandler();
handler.Initialize();
var result = handler.Load("project.als", new LoadConfig { Validate = true });
```

2. **Consistent Method Naming**
- `Create*`: Creates new objects
- `Open*`: Loads existing resources
- `Save*`: Persists to disk
- `Export*`: Converts/exports to different format
- `Merge*`: Combines multiple items
- `Get*`: Retrieves information

3. **Graceful Error Handling**
```csharp
try
{
    return AbletonProjectHandler.LoadFromFile(path);
}
catch (Exception ex) when (options.ErrorHandling == ErrorHandling.ReturnEmpty)
{
    options.Logger?.Invoke($"Error loading project: {ex.Message}");
    return AbletonProjectFactory.CreateBlankProject();
}
```

4. **Logging Support**
```csharp
options.Logger?.Invoke($"Loading project from: {path}");
```

5. **Validation and Guard Clauses**
```csharp
if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(path))
    throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(path));

if (!File.Exists(path))
    throw new FileNotFoundException("Ableton project file not found", path);
```

### Options Pattern Implementation

#### Base Options Structure
```csharp
public class ProjectOpenOptions
{
    public ErrorHandling ErrorHandling { get; set; } = ErrorHandling.ThrowException;
    public Action<string>? Logger { get; set; }
}

public class ProjectSaveOptions : ProjectOpenOptions
{
    public bool CreateBackup { get; set; } = true;
    public bool Compress { get; set; } = true;
}

public class ProjectCreationOptions : ProjectOpenOptions
{
    public string? TargetMinorVersion { get; set; }
    public int Tempo { get; set; } = 120;
    public bool CreateDefaultTracks { get; set; } = true;
}

public class ProjectMergeOptions : ProjectOpenOptions
{
    public bool PreserveColors { get; set; } = true;
    public bool MergeScenes { get; set; } = true;
    public ConflictResolution NamingConflicts { get; set; } = ConflictResolution.Rename;
}

public enum ConflictResolution
{
    Rename,      // Append number to duplicate names
    Overwrite,   // Keep first occurrence
    Skip         // Skip duplicates
}
```

### Documentation Standards
- Every public method must have XML documentation
- Include usage examples for complex operations
- Document all parameters and return values
- Explain exceptions that may be thrown
- Provide links to related methods

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Opens an existing Ableton Live project file
/// </summary>
/// <param name="path">Path to the .als file</param>
/// <param name="options">Optional settings for loading the project</param>
/// <returns>The loaded project</returns>
/// <exception cref="ArgumentNullException">Thrown when path is null or empty</exception>
/// <exception cref="FileNotFoundException">Thrown when project file is not found</exception>
/// <exception cref="InvalidOperationException">Thrown when file format is invalid</exception>
/// <remarks>
/// The project file will be decompressed automatically if needed.
/// Supports Ableton Live versions 11.0 through 12.1.
/// </remarks>
/// <example>
/// <code>
/// var sdk = AbleSharpSdk.Instance;
/// var options = new ProjectOpenOptions 
/// { 
///     Logger = msg => Console.WriteLine(msg) 
/// };
/// var project = sdk.OpenProject("myproject.als", options);
/// </code>
/// </example>
```

### Testing
- Test all public API methods
- Test with various options combinations
- Test error handling paths
- Mock file system operations
- Integration tests with actual .als files

## Best Practices

### 1. Stateless Design
- SDK singleton should not hold mutable state
- Each method should be independent
- Thread-safe by design

### 2. Backward Compatibility
- Don't remove public methods
- Use `[Obsolete]` for deprecated APIs
- Provide migration paths

### 3. Performance
- Avoid unnecessary object allocations
- Use efficient collection operations
- Consider caching for expensive lookups
- Profile common operations

### 4. Extensibility
- Design for inheritance (virtual methods)
- Support custom handlers through interfaces
- Allow dependency injection where appropriate

### 5. Validation
- Validate all inputs
- Fail fast with clear error messages
- Check file existence before operations
- Verify version compatibility

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid
- Don't expose internal library types directly
- Avoid complex method signatures (use options instead)
- Don't add UI dependencies
- Avoid global mutable state
- Don't ignore exceptions silently
- Avoid method overloads - use options pattern
